Q: Aspire 13.4.4 Blazor App-Set Custom Domain Only In Production Environment When Deploying to Azure A: <p>Here's what's going on, and a practical way to fix it.</p>
<h2>Why your check isn't working</h2>
<p><code>builder.Environment.EnvironmentName</code> (and <code>IsProduction()</code>) reflects the <strong>AppHost process's own hosting environment</strong>, which is controlled by the <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</code> / <code>DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT</code> variable set on the process that runs the AppHost.</p>
<p>When you F5/run locally, <code>launchSettings.json</code> sets that variable for you (usually <code>Development</code>), so your check works as expected in dev.</p>
<p>But when Visual Studio publishes your Aspire app, it doesn't invoke your AppHost the same way. Publish drives the AppHost through Aspire's deployment/manifest-generation pipeline (this is what <code>builder.ExecutionContext.IsPublishMode</code> is detecting), and <strong>that pipeline doesn't read <code>launchSettings.json</code> and doesn't set <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</code> to &quot;Production&quot; just because you're deploying to a &quot;production&quot; resource group</strong>. &quot;Production&quot; as a deployment target and &quot;Production&quot; as an ASP.NET Core hosting environment are two unrelated concepts here — Aspire's publish step has no built-in notion of &quot;this is my prod slot&quot; unless you tell it explicitly.</p>
<p>So during publish, <code>envName</code> is very likely coming back as something like <code>Production</code> by generic-host default (no env var set = Production is the built-in fallback) <strong>regardless of which environment you're actually targeting</strong> — which is why your <code>if</code> either never matches the way you expect, or matches every time, and you never get prompted for <code>customDomain</code>/<code>certificateName</code>. Either way, the condition isn't reliably distinguishing &quot;this specific deploy is prod&quot; from &quot;this is dev.&quot;</p>
<p>Bottom line: <strong>don't use <code>EnvironmentName</code>/<code>IsProduction()</code> to gate publish-time infrastructure logic.</strong> It's not wired up to represent &quot;which deployment target/profile I'm publishing to.&quot;</p>
<h2>The fix: drive it from something you set explicitly per publish profile</h2>
<p>Instead of trying to infer &quot;is this prod&quot; from the hosting environment, set an explicit signal yourself that only exists in your production publish profile.</p>
<p><strong>Option A — custom environment variable on the publish profile</strong></p>
<p>In Visual Studio, your publish profile (the <code>.pubxml</code> under <code>Properties/PublishProfiles</code>) lets you set environment variables for that specific profile. Add something like:</p>
<pre><code>DEPLOY_TARGET=Production
</code></pre>
<p>Then in your AppHost:</p>
<pre><code>var deployTarget = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(&quot;DEPLOY_TARGET&quot;);

if (builder.ExecutionContext.IsPublishMode &amp;&amp;
    string.Equals(deployTarget, &quot;Production&quot;, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
{
    var customDomain = builder.AddParameter(&quot;customDomain&quot;);
    var certificateName = builder.AddParameter(&quot;certificateName&quot;);

#pragma warning disable ASPIREACADOMAINS001
    app.PublishAsAzureContainerApp((infra, containerApp) =&gt;
    {
        containerApp.ConfigureCustomDomain(customDomain, certificateName);
    });
#pragma warning restore ASPIREACADOMAINS001
}
</code></pre>
<p>This only fires when you publish using the profile where you actually set that variable — no ambiguity, no reliance on the AppHost's own hosting environment.</p>
<p><strong>Option B — separate publish profiles + MSBuild property</strong></p>
<p>If you'd rather not use raw environment variables, you can define a custom MSBuild property in your dev vs. prod <code>.pubxml</code> files (e.g. <code>&lt;IsProdDeploy&gt;true&lt;/IsProdDeploy&gt;</code>) and flow it into the AppHost via an <code>EnvironmentVariables</code> item group or by reading it as a build-time constant. Same idea, just a different plumbing mechanism.</p>
<p><strong>Option C — always add the parameters, but make them optional</strong></p>
<p>If you don't want two divergent code paths at all, you could always call <code>AddParameter</code> for <code>customDomain</code>/<code>certificateName</code> but make them optional with an empty-string default for non-prod, and only call <code>ConfigureCustomDomain</code> when the value is non-empty. That way the publish profile itself (via its parameter values file) decides whether the domain gets configured, without your AppHost code needing to branch on environment at all.</p>
<h2>Why removing the code &quot;fixed&quot; the wipe, but isn't really a fix</h2>
<p>When your <code>if</code> block never actually executes during publish (because the environment check silently fails), Aspire still publishes the Container App — just without your custom domain/binding configuration. Since Aspire's Container App publishing is generally declarative (it reflects exactly what your AppHost code describes), whatever isn't explicitly configured in that publish run gets reset to defaults. That's why your bindings get wiped every time: it's not that something is <em>removing</em> them, it's that your domain-configuration code simply never ran, so the generated infrastructure has no custom domain in it at all.</p>
